Say whether the IOKit matching gate ever opens

StartIOKitMatching() calls startIOServiceMatching() and blocks in it while
kexts are probed, and no instrument has ever reported the return.  Every
panel so far has been read as "still matching" only because nothing else
appeared, which cannot be told apart from "matching finished and the next
stage prints nothing".

Two lines settle it: match go before the call, match ret after it.  With
DEBUG_BSDINIT on, the first line of bsd_init is also visible, so the two
markers together say exactly which side of the gate the boot is on.

---

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
@@ -182,7 +182,13 @@ StartIOKitMatching(void)
 {
 	SOCD_TRACE_XNU(START_IOKIT);
 	assert(gRootNub != NULL);
+	/* nio: this is the gate the boot sits in while kexts are matched, and
+	 * nothing has ever said whether it opens.  Print on the way in and on
+	 * the way out: a panel that shows match go and no match ret is still
+	 * matching, one that shows both has moved on to bsd_init. */
+	printf("\nnio: IOKit match go");
 	bool ok = gRootNub->startIOServiceMatching();
+	printf("\nnio: IOKit match ret=%d", (int) ok);
 	if (__improbable(!ok)) {
 		panic("Failed to start IOService matching");
 	}
